{"kind":"report","text":"Guarded evidence-only design review of artifact 9a52e0dcb9ceed6028571064eccfc8394f9a9f3c at workroom evidence commit b37c66e3a6289be99f67a53dac740fa0465134d2. Binding preparation confirmed evidence for planning request b5f1fb3f and no mergeability or implementation closure. The preceding ordinary report25065edf preserves the same full findings and ten source hashes; this supported guarded verdict supplies the evidence binding now available.\n\nChanges requested: immutable Draft1 of Durable browser coding across devices.\n\nReviewed evidence: attachment 2026-10-03-browser-cloud-work-draft1.md in signed event b37c66e3a6289be99f67a53dac740fa0465134d2, SHA256 9edf3ba1588fed2e2b13ad5e8f9ebcdd0abe22c28cf7ee345b494678e960f5da, 30261 bytes. All 409 numbered text lines were read. Baseline e6e6782830e0ca8a68f0d11c4d4ece5e4e98c967; a1990c9433f9d8e403d58657bdf88c2e36c58b42 remains an unapproved implementation dependency. The local-file observation originally described one extra trailing LF; planner correction 4224e6b4297ee460797071ed2fcd2447e0fe4652 reconciles it. The verdict is against the immutable attachment, not the moving plans file.\n\nThe note substantially meets the three-story scope. It connects a real dirty working tree and durable conversation to lane leases and fork credentials; gives a complete browser create/join/import/task/start/watch/steer/attention/pause/cancel/review/publication journey; and defines independent device keys of one member. It correctly separates conversation text from Room outcomes, filesystem recovery from process survival, distinct agent membership from delegation, and existing owed work from proposed production work. The six-package dependency order is workable. No schedule estimate is used to reduce functionality.\n\nRequired corrections before commissioning implementation requests:\n\n1. Decide the controlling human member's lifecycle, separately from the independent agent's membership.\nDraft 100–107 fixes an owner;203/331 says affected revoked authority stops work, while329 explicitly continues the independent agent after one human-device revocation. It does not decide what happens when the owner member is removed or loses their last active key. With the human removed and the agent member/key/lease still active, current Room admission still permits the agent (baseline packages/room/src/authority.ts:82–98 checks the agent's own member/key/role, not its controller). This is a new controller-policy decision, not a demonstrated implementation defect. State whether owner removal fences/pauses the hosted task and who may inspect or export its private retained data; separately state last-device loss, admin recovery, and any authorized owner reassignment. Reconcile the generic revocation rows with those choices. A removed member handle cannot be recovered through an invitation or set-role: admission.ts:331–349 and R-ID-5 prohibit reusing/restoring it. Same-member lost-key recovery in Story3 must explicitly concern an active member.\n\n2. Preserve read sessions as reads-only and choose the new control credential.\nDraft91–98 correctly calls for a new access amendment, but “key/session” plus a member lookup must not implicitly grant steering, pause, cancel or create. R-CRED-7 (protocol.md:595–596) makes existing sessions read-only. requests.ts:125–134 permits delegated read identity without a control-kind grant;149–171 authenticates reads and returns a member, not resource/action authorization. Choose signed resource/action-bound control requests or a separately scoped new control capability, with the proposed nonce/expiry/replay behavior. Name this decision and R-CRED-7 in work package1; current sessions remain sufficient only for authorized reads. A note-only delegation obtaining an owner's read session must not thereby control that owner's agent. Recheck revocation at dispatch and throughout watches as already proposed.\n\n3. Make the durable checkpoint, local fencing and filesystem boundary explicit.\nDraft146–162 promises saved bytes and names manifest persistence. Require complete persisted payload/Git-object closure first, then an immutable integrity-checked manifest, then a durable epoch/instance-guarded operation receipt and current-checkpoint pointer, then acknowledgment. Restore validates that closure before exposing the recovered workspace. Late callbacks from an old epoch may settle their own recorded duty but cannot install a current checkpoint, commit or receipt over a new holder/instance.\nThe existing prohibition on background writes at 151–152 is correct; define how quiescence includes children/detached processes after a shell exits. The provider spike should include one such writer rather than assuming serialized tool calls quiesce the filesystem.\nState the owned saved-root boundary: whether ignored dependencies/generated files are preserved or explicitly rebuildable, whether persistent command writes outside it are supported/refused, and how symlinks/external Git storage or unsupported special files are handled. Preserve symlink text without following it out of the owned root. At byte/output/process limits or backup failure, retain the last good checkpoint and display observed exit status separately from an acknowledged durable result. Format, incremental strategy and numerical limits can remain implementation choices. These clarify the promised complete coding experience; they do not require a browser IDE or additional providers.\n\n4. A new command attempt cannot settle the earlier unknown outcome.\nCorrect163–164: “a new authorized attempt resolves it” conflicts with177/201/343–346. A new attempt authorizes another execution, not a finding about the first execution. Give it a distinct linked ID and retain the first unknown record and reconciliation duty until definite evidence settles it. In particular, a later successful retry does not prove the earlier external write did not also occur. This is a wording/contract correction, not a runtime failure claim.\n\n5. Name the existing session-free receipt-recovery dependency.\nDraft341–346 relies on exact accepted-act replay after revocation. The pi-durable note 429–434 already names the service-binding reconnect gap. At both baseline and active a199, client/connect.ts resubmit constructs HttpWire from an HTTPS Endpoint (baseline 180–183; a199 181–184). It accepts retained declared envelopes at a199, but has no binding overload. Spike agent.ts:265–270/355–359 reconnects before replay. Choose existing HTTPS settlement or explicitly own a service-binding path that submits retained signed bytes without a fresh read session, new signature or new authority. Do not conflate disabled new dispatch with settlement of already admitted work. This is an existing public-helper dependency, not proof that an underlying wire cannot be used directly.\n\n6. Bound purge and optional-mode claims.\nDraft189–204 already retains tombstones and unsettled duties. Specify which payloads unfinished reconciliation pins, and what the same operation returns after permitted deletion: explicit expired/unavailable or still-unknown, never a fresh operation. Purging must respect referenced checkpoints/exports and the bytes needed to establish the outstanding outcome. Avoid making expiry itself proof of deletion.\nClarify whether “one hosted agent per task” means one new member/DO per task or a reusable agent identity with separate task conversations (older pi design 154–188 uses one DO per identity and one conversation per lane). Keep delegated-mode acceptance366–368 explicitly contingent on choosing that existing optional mode, unless it is intentionally part of initial delivery. Member-scoped delegation deferred at 352–354 is a different future design; do not confuse the two or quietly commission both modes.\n\nContract and source consistency:\nR-GEN-3/4/6 supports adding a device key to the same active member via an admin or recovery invitation; recovery may invite at any time but cannot itself join/delegate or do agent/lane work. The invitation is a bearer secret, not preapproval of a specific fingerprint. R-GEN-8's last-admin restriction has the recovery-key exception. Key-bound delegation dies with its grantor key. Under R-ADM-3/R-OBL-2, a separate agent member allows its human controller's qualified review; delegation instead makes that human the author. This establishes distinct Room member identities, while ordinary reviewer/checker qualification remains necessary.\nThe mock page entry at baseline packages/ui/src/main.tsx supports the note's live-entry gap. The spike README and design explicitly leave autonomous alarms, subscriptions, renew/release, real workspace credentials and UI production integration unfinished. Reuse these owed outcomes, laneJ/policy bootstrap and existing Git/Room cleanup ownership. Do not move canonical credentials or publisher/checker authority into the coding environment. Immediate controller fencing is not evidence that a dispatched remote write stopped; keep readback and cleanup duties.\n\nProvider references checked on 2026-10-03:\nCloudflare distinguishes durable sandbox identity from ephemeral instance disk, and snapshots omit processes and mounts; restoration is application managed. Its automatic-save example permits post-checkpoint loss and lists a 30-day snapshot expiry with no snapshot-delete method. These support the note's limits, not an Artroom recovery proof. [Cloudflare sandbox lifetime](https://developers.cloudflare.com/sandbox/concepts/lifetime/) and [Cloudflare automatic snapshots](https://developers.cloudflare.com/sandbox/files/save-a-sandbox-automatically/)\nDirectoryBackup saves a directory in R2, stores a hash-bearing record, requires writers stopped during backup, checks integrity before restore and provides explicit deletion; interrupted multipart uploads have their own cleanup lifetime. This supports the proposed authoritative project backup, subject to actual metadata/quiescence/abrupt-loss evidence. [Cloudflare directory backups](https://developers.cloudflare.com/sandbox/files/back-up-a-directory-to-r2/)\nMounted buckets do not provide ordinary rename/lock/permission semantics. [Cloudflare file persistence](https://developers.cloudflare.com/sandbox/files/)\nW3C WebCrypto supports CryptoKey serialization/IndexedDB storage and explicitly warns that cleared origin storage may destroy keys. Non-extractable is not immunity from hostile same-origin script using a key to sign. The identity/UI handoff should keep untrusted repository/command output inert and any future previews outside the key-owning origin. Chosen browser custody still requires bounded validation, not an indefinite-key guarantee. [W3C WebCrypto key storage](https://www.w3.org/TR/webcrypto/#key-storage) and [W3C WebCrypto author security](https://www.w3.org/TR/webcrypto/#security-considerations-for-authors)\n\nMeaningful acceptance:\nRetain the draft's real repository/host loss, lost-push reply, complete browser journey and two-device scenarios. Fold the corrections into a compact set: a checkpoint commit-boundary/old-epoch case (including a child writer); owner removal versus single-device revocation; a read-only delegated session refused control alongside an authorized control; and a second command attempt leaving the first unknown duty open. Receipt recovery after revocation and purge/expired retry are boundary outcomes, not syntactic-guard inventories. Record exact provider/version and actual commands when implementation runs, using the test-economy workflow then retained. No per-guard mutation sweep, full compile per case, install, deployment, additional first-Jam gate, or pre-review commit is required by this review.\n\nArchitecture: the new Agent control/resource contract and isolated controller belong beside the client/Room; filesystem/process persistence adds a genuine layer without replacing Room admission, lane ownership, Git publication or checker isolation.\nSecurity: the specific authority/lifecycle/checkpoint decisions above remain to be reconciled. Generic sanitized activity is not a promise that secret detection is complete (R-SEC-6); use a minimal public activity projection and keep raw transcript/command/file data within the proposed owner/admin boundary.\nSimplification: retain the four screens and a linked task panel, one initial independent-agent-member path, public/authorized existing import, existing owner-specific cleanup, and compact distinguishing witnesses. Automatic key synchronization, private host OAuth, IDE/terminal and extra providers may remain later choices as named.\n\nScope: independent design/source review with two narrow parallel reviewer reads. No application tests, compilation, installation, source edits, provider operations or deployment. No runtime security defect or shipping approval is asserted. This completes the assigned independent review; planner must reconcile these corrections and obtain review of the exact revised evidence before commissioning the new implementation work. Test-overhead request ecbc722a remains builder's highest priority; builder retains first-Jam readiness responsibility.\n","body":{"artifact":"git:sha1:589bca982736a2361d15d53f7572375ef2f7aaf3#git:sha1:9a52e0dcb9ceed6028571064eccfc8394f9a9f3c","binding":"evidence-only","head":"b37c66e3a6289be99f67a53dac740fa0465134d2","head_news_acknowledged":"[\"git:sha1:589bca982736a2361d15d53f7572375ef2f7aaf3#git:sha1:be37fa1e9ccb19fae84360a5e545ef6081d052c7\",\"git:sha1:589bca982736a2361d15d53f7572375ef2f7aaf3#git:sha1:25065edfddd2f2d291acaa26525d60dac5c1c046\",\"git:sha1:589bca982736a2361d15d53f7572375ef2f7aaf3#git:sha1:9a52e0dcb9ceed6028571064eccfc8394f9a9f3c\"]","review_frontier":"git:sha1:589bca982736a2361d15d53f7572375ef2f7aaf3#git:sha1:9a52e0dcb9ceed6028571064eccfc8394f9a9f3c","review_path":"reviewguard@1","verdict":"changes-requested"}}